#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = ["tiktoken==0.12.0"]
# ///
"""Measure real CLI text, not provider billing. Build phi/phi-server separately."""

import hashlib
import importlib.metadata
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time
import urllib.error
import urllib.request
from pathlib import Path

import tiktoken

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
BODIES = [
    """Please review the retry change in the Atlas payments service, repository
atlas/payments at commit 8f31c2a, before we merge pull request 42. Customers
occasionally receive duplicate receipts when the provider times out after accepting
a charge. The patch introduces an idempotency key derived from the checkout ID
and reuses it across retries. I attached the sanitized retry trace as retry.txt;
it contains synthetic identifiers only. Focus on whether concurrent workers can
submit different keys for the same checkout, whether expired keys permit duplicate
charges, and whether the new timeout handling preserves the original error.
Please return concrete findings with file locations, severity, and the smallest
recommended correction. Do not deploy anything. If the design looks sound, say
which failure cases you checked and what remains uncertain.
""",
    """I reviewed atlas/payments at commit 8f31c2a and the attached synthetic retry
trace for pull request 42. There is one blocking issue in src/checkout.rs around
the retry loop: the key is allocated inside each worker rather than persisted
with the checkout, so two workers can race and create different keys. Move key
creation into the checkout transaction and enforce a unique checkout ID in the
key table. The timeout branch preserves the original provider error correctly.
The provider expires keys after twenty four hours, so retries beyond that window
need reconciliation rather than another charge attempt. I checked the timeout
after acceptance, a successful retry, and two simultaneous workers against the
trace. I have not exercised a real provider sandbox or a database migration.
Please make the transaction change and add the reconciliation guard, then send
the new commit and the relevant existing test results. No deployment is needed
for this review; keep the change limited to checkout persistence.
""",
    """The requested corrections are ready in atlas/payments at commit c4d92e1,
still on pull request 42. Checkout creation now persists the idempotency key in
the same transaction as the checkout row, with a unique constraint on checkout
ID. Workers read that stored key before contacting the provider. Retries older
than twenty four hours enter reconciliation and do not create another charge.
The existing concurrency and timeout suites pass, including the simultaneous
worker case. Please review the transaction boundary and confirm that a rollback
cannot leave a worker using an uncommitted key. The migration has not been
deployed, and no live provider requests were made.
""",
    """I checked atlas/payments at commit c4d92e1 and approve the transaction
boundary in pull request 42. The key is returned only after commit succeeds;
the rollback path returns the database error before any provider call. Both
workers therefore converge on the stored key. The reconciliation guard also
prevents a fresh charge once the provider retention window has elapsed. One
remaining operational detail is the migration order: install the unique index
before starting workers that depend on it. Please include that order and the
rollback procedure in the release note. This is documentation follow up rather
than another code blocker. Production deployment still needs the normal human
release decision.
""",
    """I added the release note in atlas/payments at commit d61be70 on pull
request 42. It now requires pausing checkout workers, applying and validating
the unique index, upgrading the service, then resuming workers. If validation
fails, workers remain paused while the migration is rolled back. A service
rollback preserves persisted keys and routes expired attempts to reconciliation.
The existing test results are unchanged because this commit only updates the
release note. Please confirm that the documented order addresses your final
concern and identify any remaining merge blocker. Deployment remains pending
the release owner's approval; this message does not request production access.
""",
    """I reviewed the release note in atlas/payments at commit d61be70. The
documented sequence addresses my migration concern: workers are paused before
index validation, and neither a failed migration nor a service rollback loses
the persisted keys. I have no remaining merge blocker for pull request 42.
The review covers concurrent workers, acceptance followed by timeout, expired
provider keys, transaction rollback, and migration order. The provider sandbox
